#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163222 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$A^T = A$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$\n\nOn the other hand, the matrix\n\n$$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4880 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if $$A = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad B = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$ then the product $A\\cdot B$ is $$\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$ which is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5043 :tutorial/title "Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}]}
